@reporter 

Is it failing for the same document that you posted above as sample? i.e. the following?

```
result = await typesense
  .collections("test")
  .documents()
  .import([
    { japanese: "こんにちは" },
    { japanese: "こんにちは" },
    { japanese: "またね" }
  ], { action: "upsert" });
```

@reporter 

I've a question: how important is it that Kanji, Hiragana and Katanaka forms are "normalized" before indexing for Japanese? The problem I am running into is that the library we depend on to do this conversion is really old and unmaintained so it has a few edge cases.  I'm not even sure how accurate it is anymore given how languages evolve. 

If Typesense just indexed Japanese text as it is, would that be fine? This would mean that if there is a word indexed in Kanji, you will not be able to find it via Hiragana writing form.

